WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.617
A private AI assistant is an application that uses models, data, interfaces, and services controlled by a person or organization under defined security and privacy rules.

00:00:12.617 --> 00:00:20.188
It may run on a local computer, private server, isolated cloud instance, or hybrid architecture.

00:00:20.188 --> 00:00:23.170
A chatbot mainly exchanges messages.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:28.446
An assistant applies instructions and context to help with tasks.

00:00:28.446 --> 00:00:31.888
A knowledge assistant retrieves approved sources.

00:00:31.888 --> 00:00:38.999
An agent can select tools and perform actions, creating greater capability and greater risk.

00:00:38.999 --> 00:00:44.276
Private does not simply mean the model file is local.

00:00:44.276 --> 00:00:55.058
Prompts, documents, chat history, retrieval indexes, speech services, analytics, logs, backups, update channels, and remote access can all move or expose data.

00:00:55.058 --> 00:01:01.252
Local systems can improve control, offline capability, latency, customization, and cost predictability.

00:01:01.252 --> 00:01:07.446
Hosted services may offer stronger models, managed scaling, and less infrastructure work.

00:01:07.446 --> 00:01:12.722
Hybrid systems can route workloads according to sensitivity and capability.

00:01:12.722 --> 00:01:14.787
Control creates responsibility.

00:01:14.787 --> 00:01:27.863
The operator must size hardware, select models, maintain runtimes, secure APIs, protect data, evaluate behavior, recover failures, and decide when the assistant should defer to a human.

00:01:27.863 --> 00:01:33.598
A successful assistant is a practical system, not a model demo.

00:01:33.598 --> 00:01:43.462
It has a narrow purpose, measurable value, usable interface, grounded information, controlled access, reliable operations, and limits that users understand.

00:01:43.462 --> 00:01:46.904
Draw the trust boundary before building.

00:01:46.904 --> 00:01:54.933
Mark users, browsers, model servers, document stores, databases, tools, remote APIs, administrators, backups, and update sources.

00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:59.750
Every crossing needs an explicit data and access decision.
